{"language": "en", "duration": 98.16, "text": "You're told to quit your coffee to get rich. That's wrong. Let's do the math. A $4 coffee, 20 days a month, $80. On a $3,000 salary, that's under 3% of your budget. Even if you cut it entirely, almost nothing changes. Now look at the big categories. Housing, transportation, and food often make up more than half of a household spending. In our example, $1,000 of rent, $450 for transportation, $500 for groceries, the rent alone is more than 12 times your coffee budget. So why is this myth so widespread? Because you see the coffee go by every day. It's visible, easy to count, easy to cut. Insurance, your phone plan, your energy contract, you renew them once a year without thinking. Our brain focuses on what's small and daily, not on what's big. And this myth has a hidden cost. Guilt, you skip a small pleasure, you feel virtuous, and you never touch the real levers. So where do you act? First, on the big categories, once a year. Compare or renegotiate your insurance, your phone plan, your energy, your bank. One hour of work can beat years without coffee. Then automate your savings so you stop relying on willpower with every purchase. Finally, keep your pleasures, but choose them. If that coffee truly makes you happy, keep it. If it's an automatic reflex, cut it. Small repeated expenses do add up, but they aren't the priority. The coffee isn't your problem. The big decisions you make once a year are. And you, which expense category really weighs on you? Tell us in the comments.", "words": [{"word": "You're", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.56}, {"word": "told", "start": 0.56, "end": 0.8}, {"word": "to", "start": 0.8, "end": 1.02}, {"word": "quit", "start": 1.02, "end": 1.18}, {"word": "your", "start": 1.18, "end": 1.34}, {"word": "coffee", "start": 1.34, "end": 1.74}, {"word": "to", "start": 1.74, "end": 1.94}, {"word": "get", "start": 1.94, "end": 2.1}, {"word": "rich.", "start": 2.1, "end": 2.3}, {"word": "That's", "start": 2.8, "e